You are working on the ExamFlow project.

We are now starting:

PHASE 8 — QR CODE, CROWDMARK LINKING & EXAM ADMINISTRATION PACKAGE

The goal of this phase is to complete the physical examination
administration workflow.

The system should take the existing:

- Exam
- Student roster
- Rooms
- Seats
- Confirmed seating assignments
- Active Crowdmark exam template
- Personalized exam documents

and produce an examination administration package containing:

1. Personalized exam documents with QR codes
2. Student signature list
3. Seating map
4. Instructor/admin exam package

The final workflow should be:

Crowdmark
    ↓
Exam Template
    ↓
ExamFlow
    ↓
Roster
    ↓
Room / Seat Assignment
    ↓
Personalized Exam
    ↓
QR Code
    ↓
Signature List
    ↓
Seating Map
    ↓
Exam Administration Package

IMPORTANT:

Keep the system simple.

Do NOT recreate Crowdmark.

Do NOT implement grading.

Do NOT implement OCR.

Do NOT implement AI.

Do NOT train any AI model.

Do NOT build a new exam management platform inside Crowdmark.

Crowdmark remains responsible for exam content and grading.

ExamFlow remains responsible for:

- student identity
- seating
- personalized exam documents
- exam administration
- administrative records

==================================================
1. READ PROJECT DOCUMENTATION
==================================================

Before coding, read:

- doc/prd.md
- doc/Architecture.md
- doc/DATABASE.md
- doc/SECURITY.md
- doc/DEVELOPMENT.md
- doc/AGENTS.md

Also inspect:

- Phase 1 Authentication
- Phase 2 Exam Management
- Phase 3 Student/Roster Management
- Phase 4 Room/Seat Management
- Phase 5 Seating Assignment
- Phase 6 Crowdmark Template Import
- Phase 7 Personalized Exam Generation

Do not redesign previous phases.

Before coding, provide a concise implementation plan.

==================================================
2. CORE OBJECTIVE
==================================================

For every student:

Student
+
Exam
+
Room
+
Seat
+
Personalized Exam
+
Crowdmark reference
        ↓
QR Code
        ↓
Printed Exam

The QR code must identify the exam/student relationship safely.

==================================================
3. IMPORTANT CROWDMark PRINCIPLE
==================================================

Do NOT assume that Crowdmark supports arbitrary QR codes that automatically
associate a physical exam with a student.

Do NOT invent undocumented Crowdmark behavior.

The system must distinguish between:

A. QR code generated by ExamFlow

and

B. Official Crowdmark QR/barcode functionality.

If official Crowdmark integration is not available:

the ExamFlow QR code should be treated as an internal tracking identifier
until a supported Crowdmark integration is confirmed.

Do not claim that scanning an ExamFlow QR code automatically performs a
Crowdmark operation unless this is actually implemented and documented.

==================================================
4. QR CODE PURPOSE
==================================================

The QR code must provide a secure identifier for the personalized exam.

Recommended concept:

https://examflow.example.edu/verify/{opaque-token}

or an equivalent internal URL.

Do NOT encode sensitive student information directly in the QR code.

Do NOT encode:

student name
student ID
room
seat

directly into the QR payload.

==================================================
5. QR PAYLOAD

The QR payload should contain an opaque identifier.

Example:

examflow://exam/8f3d... 

or:

https://examflow.example.edu/e/{opaque-token}

The exact format must follow the architecture's existing URL conventions.

The token must not expose:

database sequential IDs
student ID
student name
room
seat

==================================================
6. TOKEN GENERATION

Use cryptographically secure random values.

Do NOT use:

MD5(student_id)
SHA1(student_id)
base64(student_id)
timestamp alone
incrementing database ID

as the QR security token.

Use an appropriate cryptographically secure identifier.

==================================================
7. QR TOKEN MODEL

If necessary, add fields to GeneratedExam:

qr_token
qr_generated_at
qr_version

or create a dedicated model:

ExamDocumentQr

Keep the design simple.

Do not create a complex QR management platform.

==================================================
8. QR TOKEN UNIQUENESS

The token must be unique.

Add a database uniqueness constraint.

Do not rely solely on application-level uniqueness checks.

==================================================
9. QR TOKEN ROTATION

Do not automatically rotate QR tokens.

If regeneration creates a new document version:

create a new QR token only if necessary.

Old documents must remain auditable.

==================================================
10. QR SECURITY

Scanning a QR code must not reveal sensitive student data to an unauthenticated
user.

If a public verification endpoint is needed:

return only minimal information.

Example:

"Valid Exam Document"

Do NOT display:

student name
student ID
room
seat

unless the user is properly authenticated and authorized.

==================================================
11. QR CODE LIBRARY

Use a well-maintained QR code library.

Prefer a small, mature dependency.

Do not implement QR encoding manually.

==================================================
12. QR CODE IMAGE

Generate QR code as a high-quality image suitable for printing.

The QR code must:

- have sufficient resolution
- include a quiet zone
- have good contrast
- remain scannable after normal printing
- not be placed too close to the paper edge

Do not use decorative QR designs.

==================================================
13. QR SIZE

Use a conservative physical size appropriate for printed exams.

Do not make it extremely small.

The exact dimensions should be configurable.

Example:

QR_SIZE_MM

==================================================
14. QR PLACEMENT

Place the QR code in the personalized exam header.

Example:

Student Information          QR Code

Name: Alice Student          [ QR ]
Student ID: 100000001
Room: IB101
Seat: A01

The QR code must not obscure exam content.

==================================================
15. QR + PDF GENERATION

Modify the Phase 7 document generator so the overlay can contain:

- student information
- room
- seat
- QR code

Do not rewrite the entire PDF generation architecture.

Add QR as another overlay component.

Conceptually:

Exam PDF
    +
Student Info Overlay
    +
Room/Seat Overlay
    +
QR Overlay
    ↓
Personalized Exam PDF

==================================================
16. CROWDMark REFERENCE

Use the existing optional:

crowdmark_exam_id

from Phase 6 if available.

Do not require it unless doc/prd.md requires it.

If no Crowdmark ID exists:

QR generation should still work.

==================================================
17. CROWDMark URL

If an official Crowdmark URL was stored:

do not assume it can safely be embedded in the QR code.

Prefer the internal ExamFlow QR URL.

ExamFlow can maintain the relationship:

QR token
    ↓
GeneratedExam
    ↓
Exam
    ↓
Crowdmark Exam ID

==================================================
18. QR SCANNING

Implement a protected internal verification route if required.

Example:

GET /verify/exam/{token}

The endpoint should:

- validate token
- find generated exam
- verify status
- return minimal information

Do not expose the full student record.

==================================================
19. ADMIN SCAN VIEW

If an authenticated instructor/admin scans the QR:

display:

Exam
Student
Student ID
Room
Seat
Document status

Only if the user is authorized for that exam.

==================================================
20. STUDENT-FACING SCAN

Do not assume students need to scan the QR.

The QR primarily exists for administrative identification/integration.

Do not build unnecessary student workflows.

==================================================
21. SIGNATURE LIST

Create a signature list for instructors/proctors.

Purpose:

allow staff to collect signatures in seating order.

The list should be organized according to:

Room
    ↓
Seat order

not alphabetical student order.

==================================================
22. SIGNATURE LIST DATA

Each row should contain:

Seat
Student Name
Student ID
Signature
Time

Optional if required:

Exam Version
Notes

Do not include unnecessary student information.

==================================================
23. SIGNATURE ORDER

Signature list order must follow physical seating order.

Example:

Room IB101

A01
A02
A03
A04
...

not:

Alice
Bob
Charlie
...

unless the physical seat order happens to match.

==================================================
24. MULTIPLE ROOMS

If an exam uses multiple rooms:

generate one signature list per room.

Example:

CSC108 Final

IB101 Signature List
IB102 Signature List
AC202 Signature List

Do not combine all rooms into one long list unless a summary is also useful.

==================================================
25. ROOM SUMMARY

For each room, show:

Exam
Date
Room
Total Students
Proctor

Then the signature table.

==================================================
26. SIGNATURE COLUMN

Leave sufficient physical space for a handwritten signature.

Do not make the signature column narrow.

The output is intended for printing.

==================================================
27. SIGNATURE LIST FORMAT

Use a professional printable PDF.

Default:

Letter or appropriate institutional paper size.

Use the project's configured document format.

==================================================
28. PAGE BREAKS

If a room has many students:

split across pages.

Repeat the header on each page.

Example:

CSC108 Final
Room IB101
Signature List
Page 2 of 4

==================================================
29. SEATING MAP

Generate a visual seating map for each room.

The seating map should show:

room
rows
seats
student identity

Example:

             FRONT

       A01     A02     A03     A04

       Alice   Bob     Carol   David

       A05     A06     A07     A08

       Emily   Frank   Grace   Henry

             BACK

The exact layout must reflect the actual seating configuration.

==================================================
30. SEAT POSITION

Do not assume seat labels alone define physical position.

Use the room's seating configuration from the database.

If Phase 4 stores:

row
column

use those.

If it stores:

x
y

use those.

Use the existing authoritative seat geometry.

==================================================
31. SEATING MAP ACCURACY

The seating map must reflect:

- room
- row
- column
- seat
- assigned student

Do not generate a visually attractive map that does not reflect actual
physical seating.

Accuracy is more important than aesthetics.

==================================================
32. EMPTY SEATS

Display unused seats appropriately.

Example:

A01 Alice
A02 EMPTY
A03 Bob

Do not remove empty seats from the physical map.

This allows proctors to understand the actual room layout.

==================================================
33. UNUSABLE SEATS

Seats marked unavailable should be visually distinct.

Do not assign students to them.

The map should make their status clear.

==================================================
34. STUDENT DISPLAY

For the seating map:

display:

Student Name
Student ID

or:

Student Name
Last 4 digits of Student ID

depending on the privacy requirements defined in doc/SECURITY.md.

Follow the minimum necessary information principle.

==================================================
35. MAP PRIVACY

Seating maps contain sensitive student information.

They must be protected.

Do not publish them publicly.

Do not put them in static frontend assets.

==================================================
36. MAP DOWNLOAD

Provide:

Download Seating Map

through a protected API.

==================================================
37. ADMINISTRATION PACKAGE

Create a concept called:

Exam Administration Package

It contains:

1. Exam summary
2. Room summary
3. Personalized exams
4. Signature lists
5. Seating maps

The package is an administrative output, not a new database domain.

==================================================
38. PACKAGE GENERATION

Do not create a complicated package management system.

The package can initially be generated on demand.

Conceptually:

Generate Exam Package

    ↓

/exam-package/

    exam-summary.pdf
    room-IB101-signatures.pdf
    room-IB101-seating-map.pdf
    room-IB102-signatures.pdf
    room-IB102-seating-map.pdf
    personalized-exams/

==================================================
39. BULK PACKAGE

If the package is downloaded:

prefer a ZIP archive.

Do not load all files into memory simultaneously.

Use streaming or temporary storage as appropriate.

==================================================
40. PACKAGE SECURITY

The ZIP must not contain:

- passwords
- secrets
- database credentials
- internal filesystem paths

It may contain:

- personalized exam PDFs
- signature lists
- seating maps
- exam summary

==================================================
41. ZIP FILE NAMES

Use safe names.

Example:

CSC108-Final-Exam-Administration-Package.zip

Do not include sensitive information in the package filename unnecessarily.

==================================================
42. PACKAGE CONTENT STRUCTURE

Recommended:

CSC108-Final/
    README.txt
    Exam-Summary.pdf

    Rooms/
        IB101/
            Signature-List.pdf
            Seating-Map.pdf

        IB102/
            Signature-List.pdf
            Seating-Map.pdf

    Personalized-Exams/
        100000001.pdf
        100000002.pdf
        ...

Use safe generated filenames.

==================================================
43. EXAM SUMMARY

Create a printable exam summary.

Include:

Course
Exam
Date
Start Time
End Time
Total Students
Total Rooms
Template Version

Optional:

Crowdmark Exam ID

Do not include the full student roster in the summary.

==================================================
44. PACKAGE MANIFEST

Include a simple manifest.

Example:

Exam:
CSC108 Final

Template:
v3

Generated:
2026-08-19

Students:
185

Rooms:
4

Documents:
185

Signature Lists:
4

Seating Maps:
4

This is useful for audit and verification.

==================================================
45. PACKAGE INTEGRITY

Optionally include SHA-256 hashes in the manifest for generated documents.

If implemented, do not overcomplicate it.

The purpose is verification, not cryptographic document signing.

==================================================
46. ADMIN UI

On:

/app/exams/[id]/documents

add:

[ Generate Exam Package ]

Show:

Template:
v3

Students:
185

Rooms:
4

Personalized Exams:
185

Signature Lists:
4

Seating Maps:
4

==================================================
47. PACKAGE PREVIEW

Before generation:

show:

Exam
Template
Student count
Room count
Assignment status

Then:

[ Generate Package ]

==================================================
48. VALIDATION BEFORE PACKAGE

Before generating the package:

verify:

- active template exists
- roster is complete
- all students assigned
- no duplicate assignments
- all seats valid
- rooms valid
- personalized exams available or can be generated
- no stale assignments
- no stale template

If validation fails:

do not generate the package.

==================================================
49. PACKAGE GENERATION ORDER

Recommended:

1. Validate exam
2. Validate template
3. Validate roster
4. Validate assignments
5. Generate/update personalized exams
6. Generate QR codes
7. Generate signature lists
8. Generate seating maps
9. Create manifest
10. Create ZIP
11. Calculate package hash
12. Return download

==================================================
50. IDEMPOTENCY

Generating the package twice should not corrupt existing data.

Do not create duplicate database records unnecessarily.

Package generation can be treated as an output operation.

==================================================
51. PACKAGE STORAGE

Do not permanently store every ZIP unless required.

Prefer temporary generation and controlled download.

If the package must be stored:

use protected storage and expiration/retention rules.

==================================================
52. RETENTION

Do not implement automatic deletion policies unless doc/prd.md specifies them.

However, design storage so retention can be added later.

==================================================
53. AUDIT EVENTS

Add:

QR_GENERATED
QR_SCANNED
SIGNATURE_LIST_GENERATED
SEATING_MAP_GENERATED
EXAM_PACKAGE_GENERATED
EXAM_PACKAGE_DOWNLOADED

Audit should contain:

actor
exam_id
action
timestamp
safe summary

Do not log:

QR token
student ID
student name

unless required and already approved by the security design.

==================================================
54. QR SCAN AUDIT

Record:

that a QR was scanned

but do not automatically record sensitive details.

Example:

QR_SCANNED
exam_id=internal-id
generated_exam_id=internal-id

==================================================
55. AUTHORIZATION

All administrative outputs must respect the existing authorization model.

Examples:

Instructor:

can access assigned exams.

Registrar/Admin:

can access authorized institutional exams.

Unauthorized users:

cannot download personalized exams.

Unauthorized users:

cannot download seating maps.

Unauthorized users:

cannot download signature lists.

==================================================
56. IDOR TESTING

Test:

User A:

authorized for Exam A

tries to download:

Exam B signature list

Exam B seating map

Exam B personalized exam

Exam B package

All must fail.

==================================================
57. QR IDOR

If a QR token is presented:

the token itself must not bypass authorization.

An authenticated user must still be authorized to see protected information.

==================================================
58. PUBLIC QR VERIFICATION

If public verification is supported:

return only:

"Valid Exam Document"

Do not return:

student name
student ID
room
seat

unless explicitly required.

==================================================
59. PRINT DESIGN

All administrative PDFs must be:

- black-and-white friendly
- high contrast
- readable
- conservative
- printable on standard university printers

Do not depend on color alone to communicate meaning.

==================================================
60. SIGNATURE LIST PRINT DESIGN

Use:

large enough rows
clear borders
repeat headers
room name
exam name
page number

Allow enough physical writing space.

==================================================
61. SEATING MAP PRINT DESIGN

The map must:

- fit on one page where possible
- clearly show front/back orientation
- clearly show rows
- clearly show seat labels
- clearly show students
- show empty/unavailable seats

If the room is too large:

allow a multi-page map.

Do not shrink text until it becomes unreadable.

==================================================
62. QR PRINT TEST

Test the generated QR code after:

PDF generation
printing simulation
scaling

Verify it remains machine-readable.

If possible, test with a standard QR scanner.

==================================================
63. QR ERROR CORRECTION

Use an appropriate QR error correction level for physical exam printing.

Do not maximize complexity unnecessarily.

Prefer reliable scanning over maximum data density.

==================================================
64. QR DATA MINIMIZATION

Keep QR payload small.

The QR code should reference the document rather than contain the document
data.

==================================================
65. CROWDMark FUTURE INTEGRATION

The system should maintain:

ExamFlow QR
    ↓
GeneratedExam
    ↓
Exam
    ↓
Crowdmark Exam ID

This creates a clean future integration point.

Do NOT assume Crowdmark API behavior.

==================================================
66. FUTURE CROWDMark API

If a supported official Crowdmark API becomes available later:

the integration can resolve:

ExamFlow document
    ↓
Crowdmark exam
    ↓
Crowdmark submission

This is outside Phase 8.

==================================================
67. NO GRADING

Do not implement:

grades
rubrics
marking
feedback
grading synchronization

==================================================
68. NO STUDENT PORTAL

Do not build a student portal in this phase.

The student simply receives/writes the printed exam.

==================================================
69. NO PROCTOR APP

Do not build a mobile proctor application.

A future phase could provide scanning functionality.

For now:

QR code + protected web verification is sufficient.

==================================================
70. NO REAL-TIME SEAT TRACKING

Do not implement:

live occupancy
student check-in
attendance tracking

The signature list is sufficient for this phase.

==================================================
71. SIGNATURE LIST VS ATTENDANCE

Do not treat a signature as an automatic attendance confirmation.

The signature list is a physical administrative artifact.

Do not build unnecessary attendance logic.

==================================================
72. DATABASE

Only add database structures when necessary.

Possible:

qr_token
qr_version

or:

ExamDocumentQr

Do not create database tables for generated PDFs if Phase 7 already has
GeneratedExam.

Reuse existing entities.

==================================================
73. API

Suggested endpoints:

POST
/api/exams/{exam_id}/qr/generate

GET
/api/generated-exams/{id}/verify

GET
/api/exams/{exam_id}/signature-list

GET
/api/exams/{exam_id}/seating-map/{room_id}

POST
/api/exams/{exam_id}/package/generate

GET
/api/exams/{exam_id}/package/download

Use the project's existing API naming conventions if they differ.

==================================================
74. GENERATION ENDPOINT

Before generating the package:

perform full validation.

Return a structured summary:

students
rooms
documents
signature_lists
seating_maps

==================================================
75. FRONTEND NAVIGATION

Exam detail navigation should now be:

Overview
Roster
Rooms
Seating
Template
Documents
Administration

The Administration page should provide:

- package status
- validation status
- QR status
- signature list
- seating maps
- package generation

